  "summary": "The Narmer macehead is an ancient Egyptian decorative stone mace head.",
  "description": "The Narmer macehead is an ancient Egyptian decorative stone mace head. It was found in the \"main deposit\" in the temple area of the ancient Egyptian city of Nekhen (Hierakonpolis) by James Quibell in 1898. It is dated to the Early Dynastic Period reign of king Narmer (c. 31st century BC) whose serekh is engraved on it. The macehead is now kept at the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford.\n\nThe longitudinally drilled limestone macehead is 19.8 centimeters high, has a maximum diameter of 18.7 centimeters, and weighs 8 kilograms with museum number AN1896–1908 E.3631",
